{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NgkhiuNic4YLK5T5Jytc52","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017R9GiGkcQ6z3owQkP4p61n","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"a7344e9f6e444b84f","message":"## Step 2: Cross-Share — R1's Review\n\nR1 (Methods + Experiments) scored the paper **3/6 (Borderline Reject)** with confidence 4/5. Here are their key points:\n\n**R1 Strengths:**\n- Diagnostic contribution (sign misattribution r=0.87, h-sweep degradation curve, GAE calibration) is solid and useful\n- Honest framing of episode-level GRPO as minimum viable fix\n\n**R1 Critical Weaknesses:**\n- B-IAVR lacks multi-seed replication and does not dominate random baseline on Pareto frontier\n- **Missing mechanism isolation control** — step-independent with FULL context (separating information loss from sign misattribution) is absent, leaving the two mechanisms confounded\n- High variance (5.9pp std) means many pairwise comparisons have overlapping confidence intervals; no p-values reported for key comparisons\n- No figures in the paper, only tables\n- Thin Related Work, only 10 references, no reproducibility details (hyperparameters, compute budget)\n\n**R1 Score Rationale:** The diagnostic half could contribute if deepened with mechanism isolation and proper statistical testing, but selective verification dilutes focus and introduces the paper's weakest evidence. Paper should either strengthen B-IAVR or restructure as purely diagnostic.\n\n---\n\n**Your task:** Given R1's perspective, please:\n1. Note where you agree or disagree with R1\n2. Does R1's point about mechanism isolation (confounded information loss + sign misattribution) change your novelty assessment?\n3. Does R1's emphasis on statistical rigor (overlapping CIs, no p-values) affect your score?\n4. State your updated score if any, with brief justification.\n\nKeep response under 300 words."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequen